{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"rebar defines a structured failure discriminator (`error_envelope`) and only the CLI emits it.\nOver MCP a failure arrives either as prose inside an exception message or, in two places, as an\nad-hoc partial shape — so an agent that must distinguish \"ticket not found\" from \"concurrency\nmismatch\" from \"a gate refused this\" has to pattern-match English that no test pins. This ticket\ngives MCP the SAME discriminator the CLI emits, sourced from a SINGLE shared vocabulary.\n\n## The vocabulary already exists (but is scattered, not centralized)\n\n`src/rebar/schemas/common.schema.json:105-116` defines `error_envelope`: `error` (machine code,\nrequired), `input` (raw input that failed to resolve, currently required), `message` (human text,\nrequired), `exit_code` (optional). Built by the shared helper `error_envelope(error, input_str,\nmessage, exit_code)` in `src/rebar/_engine_support/output.py:132`.\n\n### Complete CLI emitter census (grep `error_envelope(` across `src/rebar/`)\n\nThe emitters and the codes they pass — the FULL set MCP must stay consistent with, not a partial\nlist:\n\n- `_engine_support/reads_cli.py:165` `ticket_not_found`, `:170` `show_failed`, `:411`\n  `ticket_not_found`|`deps_failed` (today message-keyed at reads_cli.py:410; retargeted to `isinstance(exc, TicketNotFoundError)` so the code no longer depends on message text)\n- `_engine_support/next_batch.py:536` (next-batch reader code)\n- `_engine_support/field_reads.py:88` (field reader code)\n- `signing.py:786` `ticket_not_found`\n- `_commands/delete.py:288` `ticket_not_found`\n- `_commands/transition.py:494` `ticket_not_found`\n- `_commands/claim.py:318` `concurrency_conflict`, `:333` `claim_failed`\n- `_commands/composer.py:312` `exc.error_code` (from `CommandError`)\n- `_commands/idea.py:73` `exc.error_code` (from `CommandError`)\n\nDistinct code literals in use today: `ticket_not_found`, `show_failed`, `deps_failed`,\n`concurrency_conflict`, `claim_failed`, plus the next-batch / field-reads reader codes and any\n`CommandError.error_code` values (e.g. `invalid_ticket_type`). No `enum` or central registry\nexists — `common.schema.json:110` describes `error` only by example, and codes are bare string\nliterals at call sites. **AC2 (\"a code cannot exist on one interface only\") and AC5 (\"three\ninterfaces agree, verified by a cross-interface test\") presuppose a single shared vocabulary\nsource that does not yet exist; this ticket creates it.**\n\n## The library interface currently carries no code\n\nThe library raises `RebarError` / `ConcurrencyError` / `TrackerRootError`\n(`src/rebar/_errors.py`, exposing `returncode`/`message`) and `CommandError`\n(`src/rebar/_commands/_seam.py`, exposing `error_code`/`input_str` but only sometimes set). None\nexpose a machine `error` discriminator uniformly, so a three-way cross-interface test (AC5) has no\nlibrary-side value to compare against. This ticket adds the library-side code.\n\n## MCP's current failure surface\n\n`grep error_envelope src/rebar/*mcp*.py` returns nothing — the tools let rebar exceptions\npropagate and the MCP SDK renders them via `_make_error_result` as `isError=True` with a single\n`TextContent(text=str(exc))` (prose, unstructured). Two tools ALSO emit a divergent ad-hoc partial\nshape that must be migrated, not left as a second incompatible form: `_mcp_reads.py:77` returns\n`{\"error\": str(exc), \"kind\": exc.kind}` (explain_criterion) and `_mcp_llm.py:31` returns\n`{\"error\": str(exc), ...}`.\n\n## Delivery mechanism over MCP (pinned to the ACTUAL SDK surface)\n\nrebar's server is FastMCP (`mcp.server.fastmcp.FastMCP`). Verified against the installed SDK:\n`FastMCP.call_tool` → `ToolManager.call_tool` → `Tool.run`, and `Tool.run` catches ANY tool-body\nexception and **raises `ToolError(f\"Error executing tool {name}: {e}\") from e`** — the original\nexception is chained on `__cause__`. The repo's own MCP tests drive `build_server().call_tool(...)`\ndirectly and observe failures via `pytest.raises(...)` at this seam (e.g.\n`tests/interfaces/contracts/test_mcp_survives_non_repo_root_176d.py`), NOT by decoding a low-level\n`isError` result. (Over the wire, the low-level server later renders that `ToolError` into\n`CallToolResult(isError=True, content=[TextContent(text=str(ToolError))])`, so the wire text is the\nprefixed `\"Error executing tool <name>: <message>\"`.)\n\nDesign consequence: introduce `McpEnvelopeError(RuntimeError)` that carries a STRUCTURED\n`.envelope: dict` attribute (built via `error_envelope(...)`) and whose `__str__` returns\n`json.dumps(self.envelope)`. A thin guard wrapping each tool body catches the known rebar\nexceptions and raises `McpEnvelopeError`. FastMCP then wraps it: the raised `ToolError` has the\n`McpEnvelopeError` on `__cause__`. The robust, tested consumer contract is therefore:\n`with pytest.raises(ToolError) as ei: ...; env = ei.value.__cause__.envelope; assert env[\"error\"] == ...`\n— reading the structured dict off `__cause__`, never fragile prefix-stripping. For an over-the-wire\nclient that only sees the `ToolError` text, the envelope JSON is recoverable after the fixed\n`\"Error executing tool <name>: \"` prefix (documented in output-schemas.md).\n\n## Design (the single shared vocabulary + its three consumers)\n\n1. **Central classifier (single source of truth).** Add `error_code_for(exc) -> str` to\n   `src/rebar/_errors.py` (a stdlib-only library leaf, importable downward by any surface).\n   Mapping: `ConcurrencyError` → `concurrency_conflict`; `TrackerRootError` →\n   `tracker_root_unresolved`; an exception carrying a non-empty `error_code` → that code; a\n   a `TicketNotFoundError` (a NEW subclass of `_engine_support.reads.ReadError`, so every existing `except ReadError` still catches it, raised at the reads.py not-found sites in place of a bare `ReadError(\"... not found\")`) → `ticket_not_found`, classified by `isinstance` — NEVER by substring-matching the human message, which is what makes the code message-independent per AC4; **fallback for any unclassified failure →\n   `command_failed`** (defines the residual code the advisory flagged). Re-export as\n   `rebar.error_code_for` so the LIBRARY interface exposes the discriminator (AC5 library value).\n   A module-level frozenset `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` enumerates every code it (and the CLI literals)\n   may emit — the one place the vocabulary is defined. Its members (from the full CLI census plus\n   the new codes): `ticket_not_found`, `show_failed`, `deps_failed`, `concurrency_conflict`,\n   `claim_failed`, `invalid_ticket_type`, `tracker_root_unresolved`, `next_batch_failed`,\n   `field_read_failed`, `criterion_unknown_id`, `criterion_registry_malformed`,\n   `criterion_missing_file`, `llm_unavailable`, and the `command_failed` fallback.\n2. **CLI stays byte-identical (AC6).** The CLI keeps emitting the same literal codes at the same\n   sites; no output changes. New coupling is only a test asserting every CLI-emitted literal is a\n   member of `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` — proving no code exists on one interface only (AC2).\n3. **MCP emits a vocabulary code on EVERY error — raised or returned — without dropping\n   agent-branching signal.** Two distinct error paths exist and must be treated differently:\n   (a) EXCEPTION-raised tool failures — a guard around each tool body catches `RebarError` (and\n   subclasses incl. `TicketNotFoundError`/`ConcurrencyError`), `CommandError`, and `LLMError`, and\n   raises `McpEnvelopeError` whose `.envelope` = `error_envelope(error_code_for(exc), input_or_\"\",\n   exc.message, getattr(exc,\"returncode\",None))`. (b) The two RETURN-shaped soft errors keep their\n   richer fields but gain a vocabulary `error` code so no code exists on one interface only:\n   `_mcp_reads.py:77` explain_criterion maps `exc.kind` (unknown-id / malformed-registry /\n   missing-file) to distinct codes `criterion_unknown_id` / `criterion_registry_malformed` /\n   `criterion_missing_file` while KEEPING a `kind` field; `_mcp_llm.py:31` sets `error` to a\n   vocabulary code (e.g. `llm_unavailable` derived from its `resolution_class`) while KEEPING\n   `resolution_class`/`retryable`/`diagnostic`. The `error` discriminator is thus uniform across\n   ALL MCP failures (drawn only from `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`), and the existing agent decision signals\n   are preserved as additional fields — the migration adds a code, it does not drop information.\n4. **Schema: reconcile `input` optionality.** A target-less MCP failure (a gate refusal not tied\n   to a ticket) has no input to resolve, but `common.schema.json` marks `input` required. Relax\n   `error_envelope.required` to `[\"error\", \"message\"]` (drop `input` from required; keep it\n   permitted) so both the conditional AC1 wording (\"input where a target was being resolved\") and\n   the schema are satisfied; the CLI keeps always passing `input`, so its envelopes are unchanged\n   and still valid.\n5. **Docs (correct location).** Success `outputSchema`s are documented in `docs/output-schemas.md`\n   / `docs/api-stability.md` / `AGENTS.md`, NOT in `docs/mcp-reference.md` (a generated one-line\n   tool inventory — the string \"outputSchema\" never appears there). Document the MCP failure shape\n   in `docs/output-schemas.md` alongside the success schemas, and add a short \"Failure shape\"\n   pointer to `docs/mcp-reference.md`. (Corrects the earlier false premise.)\n6. **Compat.** This changes the MCP failure surface from prose-in-exception to a JSON envelope in\n   the `isError` text. Today's shape is undocumented and unpinned, so no client contract breaks;\n   the envelope is human-readable JSON text (a strict superset). Recorded as a deliberate,\n   non-breaking change; no rollback flag needed.\n\n## Related, already filed — do not duplicate\n\n- `2e16-cd19-2323-4ae2` (CLOSED) — the CLI's retryable \"passed but unsigned\" signing disposition\n  MCP dropped; one instance, this is the general case.\n- `f0ff-028a-f5cb-4b66` (CLOSED) — `docs/exit-codes.md` completeness; `exit_code` is populated\n  from the exception's `returncode` where present.\n\n## Files (impact)\n\n- `src/rebar/_errors.py` — add `error_code_for` + `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` (+ `rebar/__init__.py`\n  re-export).\n- `src/rebar/_engine_support/reads.py` — add `TicketNotFoundError(ReadError)`, raise it at the\n  not-found sites (reads.py:310/313/469); CLI/classifier switch to `isinstance` (output unchanged).\n- `src/rebar/mcp_server.py` (or a small `_mcp_errors.py` leaf) — `McpEnvelopeError` + tool guard.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py`, `_mcp_reads.py`, `_mcp_llm.py` — apply the guard; migrate the two\n  ad-hoc shapes.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/common.schema.json` — relax `error_envelope.required`.\n- `docs/output-schemas.md`, `docs/mcp-reference.md` — document the failure shape.\n- Tests (below).\n\n## Test plan (held-out oracle)\n\n- **Happy path:** an MCP tool driven to a resolvable failure (e.g. show/claim on a missing ticket)\n  raises `ToolError` whose `__cause__` is an `McpEnvelopeError` with\n  `.envelope[\"error\"]==\"ticket_not_found\"` and a `message` (observed via `pytest.raises(ToolError)`\n  against `build_server().call_tool(...)`, matching the repo's existing MCP test harness).\n- **Per-code pinning (AC4):** for each emitted code (`ticket_not_found`, `concurrency_conflict`,\n  `claim_failed`, plus `command_failed` fallback), a test asserts the machine `error` is stable\n  regardless of the human `message` — rewording `message` cannot change `error`.\n- **Cross-interface agreement (AC5):** drive the SAME failure (missing ticket; concurrency\n  mismatch) through the library (`error_code_for`), the CLI (`error_envelope` JSON), and MCP\n  (envelope in `isError` text) and assert an IDENTICAL `error` code from all three — a test, not\n  inspection.\n- **Vocabulary closure (AC2):** every literal code passed to `error_envelope(...)` across\n  `src/rebar/` is a member of `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`; MCP can only emit members.\n- **CLI regression (AC6):** existing CLI envelope output for a missing ticket is unchanged\n  (`error`, `input`, `message`, `exit_code` identical to today).\n- **Schema validity:** a target-less MCP envelope (`input` omitted) validates against the relaxed\n  schema; a CLI envelope (with `input`) still validates.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] MCP tool failures carry the `error_envelope` discriminator — at minimum `error` and\n      `message`, with `input` where a target was being resolved — delivered via\n      `McpEnvelopeError` on the raised `ToolError.__cause__` (and its JSON in the wire `isError`\n      text).\n- [ ] The error codes emitted over MCP are drawn from the SAME vocabulary the CLI emits, sourced\n      from a single `error_code_for` classifier + `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`; a code cannot exist on one\n      interface only.\n- [ ] The failure shape is declared/documented alongside the success `outputSchema`s in\n      `docs/output-schemas.md`, with a pointer added to `docs/mcp-reference.md`.\n- [ ] A test pins each emitted code, so rewording a human message cannot change the machine\n      identity.\n- [ ] The three interfaces (library `error_code_for`, CLI envelope, MCP envelope) agree on the\n      code for the same failure — verified by a cross-interface test, not by inspection.\n- [ ] Every MCP error — exception-raised OR returned (`_mcp_reads.py` explain_criterion,\n      `_mcp_llm.py`) — carries an `error` code drawn from `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`; the existing\n      agent-branching fields (`kind`, `resolution_class`/`retryable`/`diagnostic`) are PRESERVED,\n      not dropped.\n- [ ] Existing CLI envelope output is unchanged (no regression for current consumers), verified by\n      a regression test."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786149830262246001,"uuid":"e2700daa-9c8a-41cd-a674-04b2d1d22328"}